---
title: Multi-factor authentication
description: The three factors, how users enrol, and when MFA is demanded.
section: Configuring a tenant
---

GateKeeper supports three second factors. They are enabled **per OAuth client**, on that client's
MFA policy — so the same user can be forced through MFA on your admin console and waved through on
your marketing site.

| Factor | What the user does | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Email OTP** | Receives a one-time code by mail | Needs a working [email provider](/docs/email). Weakest of the three. |
| **TOTP** | Reads a code from an authenticator app | No dependency on email delivery. |
| **WebAuthn** | Taps a passkey or security key | Strongest. Phishing-resistant by construction. |

> [!TIP]
> WebAuthn is the one worth pushing users toward. Because the credential is bound to your origin,
> a passkey **cannot be phished** — a user on a lookalike domain simply has nothing to hand over.
> Email and TOTP codes can both be relayed to an attacker in real time by a convincing enough
> phishing page.

## Where MFA gets demanded

Two independent triggers, and it is worth understanding that they are separate:

1. **The client's policy.** If **MFA required** is on, every login to that app needs a second
   factor. Full stop.
2. **The risk engine.** Even when the policy does not require MFA, a login that scores as
   high-risk can be *stepped up* to demand it — a login from a new country, say. See
   [How risk scoring works](/docs/risk-engine).

So turning **MFA required** off does not mean "no MFA". It means "no MFA *unless the login looks
wrong*", which for most consumer products is exactly the behaviour you want.

## The login sequence

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant U as User
    participant GK as GateKeeper

    U->>GK: email + password
    GK->>GK: score the login (risk engine)
    alt MFA required, or risk is high
        GK-->>U: choose a factor
        U->>GK: code / passkey
        GK->>GK: verify
    end
    GK-->>U: authorization code
    Note over U,GK: the code is then exchanged for tokens
```

The session between the first step and the last is short-lived (10 minutes) and single-use. The
authorization code it produces lives for 60 seconds.

## Enrolment

Users manage their own factors — enrol an authenticator app, register a passkey, rename or remove a
device, and pick a preferred factor. Administrators do not enrol factors on a user's behalf.

> [!IMPORTANT]
> There is no administrator "bypass MFA for this user" switch, and no way for you to read a user's
> TOTP secret. If someone loses their only factor, the recovery path is a password reset through a
> verified email address — which is why [email](/docs/email) has to actually work before you make
> MFA mandatory.

## Timeouts

| Thing | Lifetime |
| --- | --- |
| Login session (between password and MFA) | 10 minutes |
| Email OTP | 10 minutes |
| WebAuthn challenge | 5 minutes |
| Authorization code | 60 seconds |
| Access token | 15 minutes |
| Refresh token | 30 days |
